window.docContents[18].resolve({"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-bool-and-prop":{"contents":"Lean has two different types that can play the role of truth-values. The first is\nBool, the ordinary inductive type with two elements true and false, which we\nused for the vertices of the hypercube in Constructions. The\nsecond is Prop, the type of propositions: a term p : Prop is a statement, and a\nterm h : p is a proof of that statement.\n\nThe difference is that a Bool is a value that a program can compute and branch on,\nwhile a Prop is a statement that we may or may not be able to prove. Lean provides\nthe usual Boolean operations && (and), || (or) and ! (not), each defined simply\nby matching on true and false, and bif b then x else y returns x or y\naccording to the value of b. Combining these gives expressions that #eval can\nevaluate.\n\n#eval bif !false && (false || true) then \"apples\" else \"pears\" -- \"apples\"\n\n\nLean has other Bool-valued functions such as Nat.beq m n, which checks whether\nm = n by recursion on ℕ. We can use it to write a parity test. (We mark it\nprivate, which makes it visible only in this file.)\n\n/-- Return true if n is even, false otherwise. -/\nprivate def myEven (n : ℕ) : Bool := Nat.beq (n % 2) 0\n\n\n#eval myEven 42 -- true\n#eval myEven 123 -- false\n\n\nCompare this with Mathlib's Even, which is a predicate: Even n is the statement\nthat n = r + r for some r. Nothing in this definition tells Lean how to find r,\nor how to show that no such r exists.\n\n#print Even -- def Even : {α : Type u_2} → [Add α] → α → Prop :=\n            --   fun {α} [Add α] a ↦ ∃ r, a = r + r\n\n\nMathlib states almost everything using Prop, since propositions can be quantified\nover, rewritten with, and proved by any argument at all, constructive or not.\n\nGoing from Bool to Prop is easy: Lean coerces b : Bool to the proposition\nb = true.\n\n#check fun b : Bool => (b : Prop) -- fun b ↦ b = true : Bool → Prop\n\n\nGoing from Prop to Bool is the hard direction, and this is exactly what the\ndecidability typeclass provides.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees\u0009Decidability","header":"4.1.1. Bool and Prop","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-bool-and-prop"},"/Subgraphs/#section-restricting-and-copying-partial-colourings":{"contents":"A partial colouring of s can be restricted to any subset t ⊆ s: we use the same\nfunction and note that the validity condition holds for the subset. We call this\nColoringOn.restrict.\n\n/-- Restrict C : G.ColoringOn γ s to any t ⊆ s. -/\nprotected def ColoringOn.restrict (C : G.ColoringOn γ s) (h : t ⊆ s) :\n    G.ColoringOn γ t where\n  toFun := C\n  map_rel' := fun ⟨ha, hb, hab⟩ ↦ C.valid ⟨h ha, h hb, hab⟩\n\n\nNotice that whereas Walk.copy was the cast hu ▸ hv ▸ p, which is stuck until we\nsubstitute the endpoints, here we reuse the underlying function C unchanged and\ndischarge the validity condition directly: an edge of the subgraph induced by t is\nan edge of the subgraph induced by s, so C.valid applies. Because the underlying\nfunction is untouched, a restriction is equal as a function to the original, by\nrfl and with no substitution needed. We record this as the @[simp] lemma\ncoe_restrict, it lets simp rewrite (C.restrict h) v to C v automatically, so\ndownstream proofs need not unfold restrictions by hand.\n\n@[simp]\ntheorem ColoringOn.coe_restrict (C : G.ColoringOn γ s) (h : t ⊆ s) :\n    ⇑(C.restrict h) = ⇑C := rfl\n\n\nIn practice the most common use of restrict is the trivial one where the subset is\nequal to the original set. ColoringOn is a type that encodes data, and just as with\nWalk.copy we frequently need to change the set on which a colouring is valid to one\nthat is equal but not definitionally equal to it, for example ↑(insert a s) versus\ninsert a ↑s. This is the idea behind the range copy pattern of\nPlumbing, applied, as for\nWalk.copy, to data in the type rather than in a field, and it is just restriction\nalong h.ge : t ⊆ s. We call this ColoringOn.copy. (Mathlib's Subgraph.copy is\nan instance of the pattern itself: it replaces the verts and Adj fields of a\nsubgraph by propositionally equal ones.)\n\n#check Subgraph.copy\n\n\n/--\nConstruct a G.ColoringOn γ t from C : G.ColoringOn γ s and proof that s = t -/\nprotected\ndef ColoringOn.copy (C : G.ColoringOn γ s) (h : s = t) : G.ColoringOn γ t :=\n  C.restrict h.ge\n\n\n@[simp]\ntheorem ColoringOn.coe_copy (C : G.ColoringOn γ s) (h : s = t) : ⇑(C.copy h) = ⇑C :=\n  rfl\n\n\nThe pointwise form. It follows from coe_copy under simp, so it is not itself a\nsimp lemma; we keep it as a named lemma for the explicit rw chains that step\nthrough a copy to expose the underlying union/insert.\n\nlemma ColoringOn.copy_apply (C : G.ColoringOn γ s) (h : s = t) {v : V} :\n    (C.copy h) v = C v := rfl\n\n\nBy far the most common source of such mismatches for us will be due to the coercion\nfrom Finset V to Set V. Following the Mathlib convention that definitions are\nmade for the more general type, our basic constructors are stated for Set V. In\npractice, however, we almost always colour finite sets. If we pass ColoringOn a\nFinset V, then it is coerced to a Set V (since Finset has a SetLike\ninstance). Depending on how this coercion is applied we may end up with a type\nmismatch that we will need to fix with copy.\n\nFor example, we might end up with a colouring of ↑s ∪ ↑t (the Set V given by\ncoercing each of s t : Finset V to Set V and then taking their union) when the\ngoal asks for a colouring of ↑(s ∪ t) (the coercion of the Finset V that is the\nFinset union of s and t). These two sets are obviously equal but the proof\nis not rfl (it is the simp lemma Finset.coe_union).\n\n/- error: Type mismatch -/\nexample (s t : Finset V) [DecidableEq V] (C : G.ColoringOn γ (↑s ∪ ↑t)) :\n    G.ColoringOn γ ↑(s ∪ t) := C\n\n\nexample (s t : Finset V) [DecidableEq V] (C : G.ColoringOn γ (↑s ∪ ↑t)) :\n    G.ColoringOn γ ↑(s ∪ t) := C.copy (by simp)\n\n\nFor this reason we will need to use copy frequently in proofs. Fortunately\nFinset.coe_insert, Finset.coe_union, Finset.coe_empty etc are all simp\nlemmas, so the proof obligation that copy requires will almost always be discharged\nby simp.\n\n#check Finset.coe_empty  -- ((∅ : Finset α) : Set α) = ∅\n#check Finset.coe_insert -- ↑(insert a s) = insert a ↑s\n#check Finset.coe_union  -- ↑(s₁ ∪ s₂) = ↑s₁ ∪ ↑s₂\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs\u0009Partial Colourings*","header":"9.6.1. Restricting and Copying Partial Colourings","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-restricting-and-copying-partial-colourings"}});